    {
      "kind": "lede",
      "html": "<p>An HSE inspector walks onto your site and asks for the scaffold inspection record. You cannot find it, so you cannot show the inspection was done. This is what a <strong>construction site inspection report</strong> must contain under UK law, and what it costs when the paperwork is missing.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "takeaways",
      "heading": "The short version",
      "html": "<ul><li>CDM 2015 puts the duty to manage and monitor site safety on the principal contractor. Inspection records are the proof.</li><li>Inspect scaffold before first use, after anything that affects it, and at least every seven days.</li><li>Schedule 7 of the Work at Height Regulations 2005 sets the minimum content of a scaffold report.</li><li>No current record means no proof the inspection was done. An inspector can serve a prohibition notice where an activity risks serious personal injury (Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, section 22).</li><li>A general site report covers welfare, access, edge protection, plant, electrics and fire.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The duty",
      "heading": "CDM 2015 puts monitoring on the principal contractor",
      "html": "<p>The Construction (Design and Management) Regulations 2015 give the principal contractor a clear job: plan, manage, monitor and coordinate health and safety through the construction phase. That means inspections happen and the results are written down.</p><p>CDM 2015 does not set how often for every item. The principal contractor weighs the risk. The construction phase plan must set out the health and safety arrangements and site rules (regulation 12(2)). The record is the proof.</p><p>The principal designer owns the pre-construction phase. When the site goes live, the principal contractor carries the safety duty. If one firm is both, both duties apply.</p><p>For notifiable projects, those over 30 working days with more than 20 workers at once, or over 500 person-days, tell HSE before work starts. The records feed the health and safety file for the client.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Scaffolding",
      "heading": "When a scaffold must be inspected",
      "html": "<p>The Work at Height Regulations 2005 are clear on scaffolding used as a place of work. Regulation 12 needs an inspection in position before first use. Regulation 12(4) says a working platform used for construction work, from which a person could fall 2 metres or more, must not be used unless it has been inspected in that position within the previous 7 days (a mobile platform may be inspected on the site). Under regulation 12(3) it is also inspected after exceptional circumstances that could jeopardise its safety, such as high winds or a vehicle impact.</p><p>A competent person does each inspection. The inspector prepares the report before the end of the working period and gives a copy to the person who asked for the inspection within 24 hours (regulation 12(7)). It stays on site until the scaffold comes down. The employer then keeps it for three months.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "Schedule 7",
      "heading": "What a scaffold report must contain",
      "html": "<p>Schedule 7 sets the minimum. Every line is a must.</p><ul><li>Name and address of the person the inspection was for</li><li>Where the working platform is, and what it is</li><li>Date and time</li><li>Anything that could risk health or safety</li><li>Action taken</li><li>Further action needed</li><li>Name and position of the person making the report</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "The wider walk",
      "heading": "What a general site report covers",
      "html": "<p>Beyond scaffold, a general report covers the whole site. There is no single set format, but most use the same items.</p><ul><li>Welfare: toilets, washing, drinking water, rest area</li><li>Housekeeping: waste, clear routes, materials storage</li><li>Access: site entrance, people and vehicles kept apart</li><li>Edge protection: barriers at openings, floor holes, roof edges</li><li>Excavations: support, edge protection, ladders, spoil</li><li>Plant: condition, operator certificates, exclusion zones</li><li>Electrics: temporary supplies, cables, RCDs in use</li><li>Fire: extinguishers, escape routes, hot work controls</li><li>PPE: available and worn</li><li>Subcontractors: controls in place, kept apart from other trades</li></ul><p>A weekly inspection by the site manager or safety officer, with daily informal checks, is normal on medium projects. The weekly one gives the written record.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "heading": "Free site inspection report template",
      "html": "<p>A construction site inspection report holds the same six parts every time: header, scope, method, findings, actions with an owner and a date, and sign-off. The <a href=\"/pages/inspection-report-template-uk.html\">free inspection report template</a> sets them out for any trade, and <a href=\"/pages/how-to-write-site-inspection-report-uk.html\">how to write a site inspection report</a> shows a weak and a strong finding side by side.</p><p>For a scaffold, add the Schedule 7 particulars listed above. Quickler writes the report from the walk you run in WhatsApp. Graeme Clark at Ethos Safety went from 1 to 2 hours of write-up to 14 minutes on average.</p>"
    },
