      "html": "<p>The problem is timing. Notes on a clipboard get typed up later. Detail leaks away in the gap.</p><p>Tablet forms fix part of it. They add battery, patchy signal and a new screen to learn.</p><p>With Quickler you run the walk in WhatsApp, the app already on every phone on site. Photos go in the chat, timed. No typing up afterwards. See <a href=\"/pages/how-to-write-site-inspection-report-uk.html\">how to write a site inspection report</a> or the <a href=\"/pages/construction-daily-report-app-uk.html\">construction daily report app</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "kind": "default",
      "eyebrow": "When records slip",
      "heading": "No current record means no proof of inspection",
      "html": "<p>An HSE inspector can ask for scaffold records at any time. If the last report is missing or out of date, you cannot show the inspection was done.</p><p>An inspector can serve a prohibition notice where an activity involves a risk of serious personal injury (Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974, section 22). It directs that the activity must not continue until the matters are put right. A lost day on a live job is rarely cheap.</p><p>After a serious incident, missing records are not an admin slip. They suggest safety was not managed. Prosecution under the Work at Height Regulations 2005 or the Health and Safety at Work etc. Act 1974 is possible. In civil claims, no records makes it harder to show you took reasonable care. The fix is simple: a current record you can show on demand. For specialist work, see <a href=\"/pages/structural-engineer-report-software-uk.html\">structural engineer report software</a>.</p>"
    },
