    {
      "kind": "faq",
      "heading": "Questions, answered",
      "html": "<h3>How often must scaffolding be inspected?</h3><p>Under the Work at Height Regulations 2005, a scaffold used as a place of work is inspected before first use, after anything likely to affect it (such as high winds or an impact), and at least every seven days while in use. Each inspection is recorded. The report is kept until the scaffold comes down.</p><h3>Who can inspect a scaffold?</h3><p>A competent person. That means enough training, experience and knowledge to spot hazards and judge risk. No set qualification is required. Many firms use a CISRS-qualified inspector or a trained site manager.</p><h3>What must a scaffold report contain?</h3><p>Schedule 7 sets the minimum. Who the inspection was for, with address. Where the platform is, and what it is. Date and time. Anything that could cause a risk. Action taken. Further action needed. Name and position of the person who wrote it.</p><h3>What if an inspector asks and you have no record?</h3><p>With no current record you cannot show the inspection was done. An inspector can then serve a prohibition notice or an improvement notice, or prosecution can follow. After a serious incident, missing records make your position much worse.</p><h3>Is there a free construction site inspection report template?</h3><p>Yes. Our free inspection report template covers the six parts every site report needs. For a scaffold, add the items Schedule 7 of the Work at Height Regulations 2005 lists. Quickler can also fill your own template from a WhatsApp walk.</p>"
    }
